{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nParent epic `a4bd-4ca8-c44f-49bb`; depends on S1 (`248f-ac00-18db-4259`, closed). S3\n(`47a0-7019-da4b-40e0`) makes the inbound removal path decline removals of links carrying no\npeer-confirmation record. Without a backfill, the FIRST post-upgrade pass would find an empty\nstore and decline EVERY legitimate peer deletion — a regression worse than the blind spot being\nfixed. The epic states it directly: \"Backfill grandfathers existing managed links as\nconfirmed-at-upgrade (marked as such) so the first post-upgrade run does not decline every\nlegitimate removal.\"\n\nThis story must land BEFORE S3 turns the decline on.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `peer_confirmations.py` — add `DIRECTION_BACKFILL = \"backfill\"` beside the existing\n  `DIRECTION_OUTBOUND` / `DIRECTION_SNAPSHOT`. `SOURCE_BACKFILL` already exists; the direction\n  vocabulary must stay a closed, named set rather than gaining a bare string literal.\n- `peer_confirmations.py` — the loader records whether the store file was ABSENT at open (a\n  `was_absent` attribute set in `PeerConfirmationStore._load`). Backfill runs on \"file was\n  absent\" (a fresh upgrade), NOT on \"store is empty\": an operator who legitimately emptied the\n  store must not silently have it re-populated.\n- `peer_confirmations.py` — add\n  `backfill_from_managed_refs(store, local_tickets, binding_store, pass_id) -> int`.\n  For each local ticket, read its managed refs via\n  `rebar.reducer._managed_refs.managed_ref_set(ticket)` — its kinds are already constrained to\n  `MANAGED_REF_KINDS`, so no extra kind filter is needed or wanted. For each `(kind, target)`\n  whose `target` resolves to a BOUND peer key via `binding_store.get_jira_key(target)`, write a\n  record keyed `(ticket_local_id, target, kind)` with `source_kind=SOURCE_BACKFILL`,\n  `direction=DIRECTION_BACKFILL`, `link_id=None`, `confirmed_pass=pass_id`.\n\n**The no-downgrade guard is explicit, because `record()` will not provide it.** S1's landed\n`PeerConfirmationStore.record()` overwrites its key UNCONDITIONALLY — it reads the previous\nentry only to preserve `first_confirmed_at`. So backfill MUST check first and write ONLY when\n`store.get(source, target, kind) is None`. Any existing record (`push` or `snapshot`) is\nstrictly stronger evidence than a grandfather assumption and must be left untouched. This\nread-before-write skip is the entire mechanism behind AC5's no-downgrade half; the upgrade half\nneeds no new code, since a later `push`/`snapshot` `record()` call overwrites a backfilled entry\nby the same unconditional behaviour.\n\n- `reconcile.py::_persist_and_log` — invoke backfill inside the existing `if persist:` block,\n  immediately BEFORE the existing `_confirm_peer_links(ctx, pass_id)` call (S2's snapshot\n  seam, now on main at `reconcile.py:597`), so a genuine snapshot observation in the SAME pass\n  upgrades a backfilled record's provenance rather than being blocked by it. Wrap it in the same\n  fail-open `try/except Exception` shape the neighbouring steps use (log to stderr, never break\n  a sync pass).\n- A backfilled record is treated as confirmed by `is_confirmed()`. Distinguishing backfill from\n  proof at the DECISION point is deliberately NOT done — that would re-open the blind spot for\n  every pre-upgrade link, which is exactly what grandfathering exists to avoid.\n- `managed_refs` monotonicity is unchanged; nothing is pruned.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1 On a store whose file was absent, every managed ref with a bound target gets a record\n      with `source=\"backfill\"` and `direction=\"backfill\"`; the store is then non-empty and\n      persisted. Proof:\n      `pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_peer_confirmations_backfill.py -k fresh_upgrade`.\n- [ ] AC2 A managed ref whose target is UNBOUND gets no record. Proof:\n      `pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_peer_confirmations_backfill.py -k unbound`.\n- [ ] AC3 Backfill does NOT run when the store file existed but is empty. Proof:\n      `pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_peer_confirmations_backfill.py -k existing_empty`.\n- [ ] AC4 Backfill does not re-run once the file exists — a second pass adds zero records. Proof:\n      `pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_peer_confirmations_backfill.py -k second_pass`.\n- [ ] AC5 Provenance never downgrades: backfill SKIPS a key already carrying a `push` or\n      `snapshot` record (asserted by inspecting `source` after backfill runs over it), while a\n      later snapshot confirmation DOES overwrite a backfilled key's `source` to `\"snapshot\"`.\n      Proof:\n      `pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_peer_confirmations_backfill.py -k provenance`.\n- [ ] AC6 `is_confirmed()` returns True for a backfilled record. Proof:\n      `pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_peer_confirmations_backfill.py -k confirmed`.\n- [ ] AC7 In no-write mode (`persist` False) no backfill is written, and a raising backfill does\n      not break the pass. Proof:\n      `pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_peer_confirmations_backfill.py -k \"nowrite or fail_open\"`.\n- [ ] AC8 Backfill runs BEFORE `_confirm_peer_links` in the persist sequence. Proof:\n      `pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_peer_confirmations_backfill.py -k ordering`.\n- [ ] AC9 Every edited module stays under the 800-line cap. Proof: `bash -c 'git archive HEAD |\n      tar -xO src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/reconcile.py | wc -l'` per edited file, < 800.\n- [ ] AC10 Gates green. Proof: `make lint` exit 0, `make typecheck` exit 0,\n      `pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler` exit 0.\n\n## Testing\n\n- New suite `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_peer_confirmations_backfill.py` covering the\n  fresh-upgrade backfill, unbound-target skip, existing-but-empty non-run, one-shot behaviour\n  across two passes, both halves of the provenance rule, `is_confirmed` acceptance, the no-write\n  and fail-open paths, and the backfill-before-snapshot ordering.\n- Regression: `make lint && make typecheck && pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler`.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nThe removal decline (S3). No pruning of `managed_refs`. No change to its monotonicity or to\n`record()`'s unconditional-overwrite contract."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786573798041694001,"uuid":"6c5d64c7-a805-4116-808a-3169e14baf89"}